You are absolutely right, and good catch. Keeping vPlanKO as a variable is exactly how it should be built so you can easily add other planning codes (like S02) later.

The reason it failed is due to a very common Qlik quirk with how variables evaluate strings.

If your script defines the variable using LET like this:
LET vPlanKO = 'S01';

Qlik evaluates the expression, strips the single quotes, and stores exactly S01 in the variable. When your script later expands it via $(vPlanKO), it injects Match(KO, S01), which causes Qlik to look for a field named S01 and crash.

The Fix
To preserve the quotes so they survive the expansion into the Match() function, you should change how the variable is declared at the top of your script.

Use SET instead of LET, and include the quotes. SET treats the right side as a literal string without evaluating and stripping the quotes.

Change your variable definition to this:

qlik
SET vPlanKO = '''S01''';
(Note: If you want to add multiple codes right now based on the traces, you can format it like this: SET vPlanKO = '''S01'', ''S02''';)

