Gemini replied
The NPlan Logic Explained
The NPlan (Number of Planning phases) logic is a counter designed to tell us exactly how many jobs in your dataset were "infected" by these long, zero-hour planning phases.

Here is exactly how it works in the script step-by-step:

1. Evaluate every single phase (Row by Row)

qlik
If(Match(KO,$(vPlanKO)) or WildMatch(PhTitle,$(vPlanTitle)), 1, 0)
For every phase, Qlik checks: Is this KO code in our bad list? OR is this Title in our bad list? If either is true, it assigns a 1. If it is actual production work, it assigns a 0.

2. Roll up to the Job (ICN) Level

qlik
Sum(...) AS NPlan
... GROUP BY ICN
Because this is grouped by ICN, Qlik adds up all the 1s for that specific job. If NPlan is 0, it means the job was 100% pure production. If NPlan is 3, it means the job had 3 planning phases hiding inside it.

3. Count the Affected Jobs

qlik
Sum(If(NPlan>0,1,0)) AS WithPlan
Finally, in the diagnostic summary, we count how many total ICNs had an NPlan greater than 0. This is what generates the final trace line: ICNs containing a suspected planning phase = X of Y. It proves exactly how much of your historical data was being skewed by tracking/planning overhead.

